useum could also be a  kind of healing place  even in europe  germany's decision to act marks a  paradigm  shift in the handling of looted  artifacts  and i'm pleased to welcome the  senegalese philosopher professor soliman  bashed yang  on to the program to talk about this he  is currently the director of the  institute of african studies at columbia  university in  new york welcome professor jan lovely to  have you on the program  so this development is being called a  game changer give us your reaction to  the news  that germany will start returning benin  bronzes to nigeria next year  well it is great news indeed because you  you said it  right this is a game changer already  the the decision by the french president  to restitute  certain objects was a groundbreaking  decision but this is a huge huge game  changer  because the benin bronzes have had this  hugely symbolic value yeah professor in  your work you you've talked about that  how the brilliant bronzers are  particularly important tell us what  makes them so special  well uh when they were looted in 1897  in a way europe discovered them more  more broadly  and the benin bronzes really changed  the perception that europeans had of  african art  they were just fascinated by the  sophistication of the work  they were really  stunned astonished at first by  what it meant in terms of mastery  of metal work and in particular  the very sophisticated technique uh  known as  lost wax uh casting  meaning that you you build a figure  in wax and then you pour the melted  metal into it and when it  when it's uh cold it takes the form of  whatever it is that you you  you carved out of wax and  so that's the the a very important  aspect  in the first place that is on the  european side it meant for the  perception of african art  and then there is the fact that the way  in which they were  acquired acquired is really a euphemism  they were  looted they were actually taken by  violence  from uh the the palace of the of 